WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
In the Scary Stories series, few&nbsp;
creatures have scared their way so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.580
firmly into our collective conscience as Harold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:09.480
The gazing scarecrow that came to life and&nbsp;
wreaked a horrid revenge on its creators.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.680
Thomas and Alfred were cow herders who, in a bid&nbsp;
to stave off boredom, slung together some old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:18.300
sacks stuffed with straw to make a man-sized doll.&nbsp;
They named this doll “Harold” after another farmer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:22.740
that they disliked and began abusing it with&nbsp;
curses, kicks, and smearing food on its face.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:27.300
Then, one day, Harold grunted. Thomas&nbsp;
and Alfred were cautious, temporarily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:31.620
but eventually returned to their ways, treating&nbsp;
Harold as a punching bag. Then one day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:35.880
Harold stood up, climbed to the roof of their&nbsp;
hut, and began trotting back and forth. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:41.040
unsure of what Harold was up to, Thomas and&nbsp;
Alfred became uneasy. All night Harold marched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:44.760
back and forth on the roof of the hut. By&nbsp;
day, Harold would climb down and stand in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:48.540
a corner of the pasture for hours. Thomas and&nbsp;
Alfred decided it was time to get out of there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:52.080
but when they had only gone a mile away,&nbsp;
they realized they were missing a milk

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:56.580
stool. Assuming the living doll couldn’t be&nbsp;
of actual, real, danger, Thomas went back for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:00.900
the stool while Alfred waited. When Thomas didn’t&nbsp;
return, Alfred went back in search of his friend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:05.820
only to find Harold on the roof, laying a&nbsp;
fresh, flayed, human skin out to dry in the sun.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:09.720
It may be hard to believe, but that&nbsp;
was the toned-down take on Harold. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:13.020
learn about how the horror of this old&nbsp;
European folk tale has inspired living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:16.860
doll legends for hundreds of years,&nbsp;
stick around to the end of this video.

00:01:17.088 --> 00:01:21.816
(metal music)

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:24.600
Welcome to Horror History.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:29.220
You may know Harold from the story “Harold”&nbsp;
included in the book Scary Stories 3: More&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:33.180
Tales to Chill Your Bones, the third collection&nbsp;
in the popular Scary Stories series written by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:37.620
author Alvin Schwartz. In today’s lesson, we’ll&nbsp;
be exploring Harold’s old European roots, and how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:42.180
they relate to his legendary counterparts like the&nbsp;
Toggel, Golem, and even Frankenstein’s monster.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:45.780
“Harold”, is one of the more recognizable&nbsp;
characters from the Scary Stories series.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:50.580
Though only referred to as a doll in the text, cow&nbsp;
herders Thomas and Alfred do place Harold outside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:54.300
to scare away birds and the illustration&nbsp;
from Stephen Gammel is more reminiscent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:58.080
of the traditional scarecrow. In Gammel’s&nbsp;
signature style ink-stained illustration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:02.520
Harold stands in a field, tied to a pole, with&nbsp;
his legs spread in a wide stance holding up a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:06.480
sagging body dressed in old clothes. Thus,&nbsp;
Harold is often referred to and depicted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:10.440
in modern retellings as a scarecrow but, as&nbsp;
we’ll cover more in-depth later in the video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:14.280
in old European folklore, a living&nbsp;
man-sized murder doll is a murder doll:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:18.900
whether it’s stuffed with hay, made of clay,&nbsp;
or a robot that returns for Judgement Day.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:23.520
The Scary Stories trilogy books were collections&nbsp;
of short horror stories adapted for kids that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:27.840
were released in the early 1980s and 90s. The&nbsp;
frightening plots and macabre illustrations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:31.380
were the oft-cited reasons for banning the&nbsp;
books in many schools. The stories in each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:36.120
collection are inspired by, if not a retelling&nbsp;
of existing urban legends, historical accounts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:41.160
or folklore. Keep in mind, the original material&nbsp;
for most commonly known legends today are usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:45.300
pretty rough. Even the Grimm fairy tales&nbsp;
version of Cinderella featured the stepsisters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:50.040
cutting off parts of their feet to fit the glass&nbsp;
slipper and eventually being blinded by birds.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:54.060
Death, disfigurement, and blood are common&nbsp;
occurrences in these Harper-Collins books&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:58.800
for ages 9 and up. The third installment alone&nbsp;
features dismemberment, rabid sewer rats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:00.960
and literal Death (like, the guy).

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:06.120
Scary Stories 3: More Tales to Chill your Bones,&nbsp;
first released in 1991 and introduced a few new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:10.680
characters that would be remembered decades later.&nbsp;
“The Red Spot” is a classic urban legend retelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:15.300
that provides a good eight-legged reason not to&nbsp;
pick at a zit. “The Dream”, inspired by Victorian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:19.860
ghost stories, introduced the benevolent thin&nbsp;
and wide smile of the mysterious Pale Woman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:24.960
or Pale Lady. And Scary Stories 3: More Tales&nbsp;
to Chill Your Bones is where we find Harold.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:27.720
Preceding the story, the&nbsp;
introductory splash page reads:

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:31.740
“You will say that these stories could&nbsp;
not happen. Yet some say they did happen.”

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:34.800
If something like “Harold” did happen,&nbsp;
we’re going to have to look deep into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:38.760
the scant details of the story to figure&nbsp;
out his origin. And considering a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:42.180
the variants of this story also end&nbsp;
with someone being skinned or at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:46.020
very least dead, we better start right&nbsp;
now. Enough talking, roll the thing.

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:49.680
(mysterious music)

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:55.800
The story “Harold” contains pretty broad details&nbsp;
but the choices are specific. The names “Thomas”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:59.760
“Alfred”, and “Harold” are all of old English&nbsp;
origin and came into popularity around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:04.320
middle ages or before the 15th century. Thomas and&nbsp;
Alfred are described in the text as cow herders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:07.680
who traveled from the valley to the mountains&nbsp;
where they lived in a “hut”. The old English,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:11.434
Germanic, and Norwegian roots of the word&nbsp;
“hut” also date back to the middle ages.

00:04:11.434 --> 00:04:14.901
This points to the story&nbsp;
happening pre-16th century in central Europe.

00:04:14.901 --> 00:04:16.799
Then of course there’s the skinning of&nbsp;
Thomas.

00:04:16.799 --> 00:04:22.860
While human skinning has been practiced by many cultures at many periods of&nbsp;
time (for various surely reasonable reasons),

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:26.280
the flaying of humans for punishment was&nbsp;
particularly popular during the middle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:30.780
ages / medieval period, which was from the&nbsp;
5th century to the 15th century respectively.

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:34.860
The only overlap for all of the elements&nbsp;
would be the 15th century in central Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:38.351
so I’m gonna say it’s set around&nbsp;
then. Good thing for human skinning!

00:04:38.351 --> 00:04:40.067
–  Closer.

00:04:40.067 --> 00:04:41.640
*CLEAR THROAT* – But this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:44.940
doesn’t answer the really pressing&nbsp;
question of what is Harold?

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:49.620
In the story, there’s no voodoo incantation,&nbsp;
demonic possession, or ventriloquist’s curse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:54.000
before the “doll” comes to&nbsp;
life. Harold isn’t possessed by a spirit and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:56.820
as far as we can tell, isn’t&nbsp;
stuffed with living insects.

00:04:56.820 --> 00:05:00.720
Harold is seemingly willed to existence by&nbsp;
Thomas and Alfred despite neither of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:05.700
displaying any sort of supernatural ability. But&nbsp;
this is folklore. Most mass-printed books and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:09.900
films follow more of a fairy tale structure,&nbsp;
in which some form of explanation is usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:13.560
given for the strange occurrences in the&nbsp;
story; along with a more positive ending.

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:18.600
Folklore, while also being entertainment, was and&nbsp;
is more of a means of passing down customs and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:23.580
traditions. A local legend, even when unpolished&nbsp;
and formless, is a report of a weird, frightful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:29.280
event, like Mothman in West Virginia or The Jersey&nbsp;
Devil of… well Jersey. The stories don’t always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:33.720
have a linear narrative direction, though they’ll&nbsp;
often follow similar patterns to a common lesson.

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:38.460
In this case, that “lesson” seems more clear than&nbsp;
the actual reason that Harold comes to life. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:42.060
scarecrow is named after a farmer that the two&nbsp;
main characters don’t like, and they use it as an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:46.080
indirect way to disrespect him. It’s pretty clear&nbsp;
that the lesson is to treat others with respect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:49.920
even when they’re not around, and from there, I&nbsp;
would infer that Harold’s incarnation probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:53.700
represents the anger of this farmer, that&nbsp;
Thomas and Alfred have most likely mistreated.

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:57.300
But remember, the Scary Stories passage&nbsp;
is only one interpretation of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:00.840
legend. Author Alvin Schwartz was an avid&nbsp;
researcher, whose goal with the series was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:05.160
to inspire the reader to search out folklore&nbsp;
on their own. Which I am now doing for you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:09.900
so mission accomplished. An extensive Notes and&nbsp;
Sources section is included at the back of every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:14.520
Scary Stories collection, sourcing everything&nbsp;
from college-reviewed essays to Life magazines.

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:18.480
According to the resources included in Scary&nbsp;
Stories 3: More Tales to Chill your Bones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:20.940
Harold is a retelling of a Swiss-Austrian legend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:23.760
The Tunsch Baptized: by the Cowherds&nbsp;
of Göschenen.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:28.500
In a mountain pasture behind Göschenen, a village&nbsp;
in northern Switzerland, several servants leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:33.540
a dissolute life put some odds and ends together&nbsp;
into a Tunsch, also called a Toggel, or a:

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:35.280
Tunggel
Dittitolgg&nbsp;

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:37.080
Dittitunsch
Tschungg

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:41.700
Like Thomas and Alfred, the servants played pranks&nbsp;
and smeared food on the doll until they tried to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:45.780
baptize it, and they were shocked by the doll&nbsp;
coming to life. The servants recovered quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:50.220
and returned to tormenting their Toggel. Then the&nbsp;
Toggel climbed up and began trotting around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:54.000
roof, and the rest is exactly like “Harold”.&nbsp;
Come Autumn, the servants left the pasture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:57.420
but they accidentally left a milking&nbsp;
stool behind. One was sent back for it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.420 --> 00:07:01.080
and when they looked back on him, they saw&nbsp;
his skin stretched across the roof of the hut.

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:04.380
There are other variants of the “living&nbsp;
doll” tale around central Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:08.760
but pinpointing the exact origin of this&nbsp;
story may be impossible since this folklore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:12.060
is descended from oral tradition;&nbsp;
stories passed down by being retold.

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:15.480
Swiss folklore is full of legendary&nbsp;
creatures that were indicative of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:19.260
the environment and culture, like Frost&nbsp;
Giants, the personification of avalanches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:23.340
and Jack Frost, the personification of&nbsp;
winter conditions. A figure of Alpine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:26.970
folklore who’s received a resurgence in&nbsp;
popularity is the Christmas demon Krampus.

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:31.800
In “Harold”, Thomas and Alfred create a figure for&nbsp;
their own entertainment. When Harold shows signs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:36.360
of life, they only stop briefly before continuing&nbsp;
to abuse him. By the time they take him seriously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:40.980
it’s already too late. Had the two not followed&nbsp;
their hubris into believing they had nothing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:44.580
fear from Harold, Thomas might not be a&nbsp;
lampshade in a living scarecrow’s hut.

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:49.080
There’s a phrase associated with this story&nbsp;
trope: “Death by one’s own horse”. Where man is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:54.360
the greatest danger to himself. In the isolated&nbsp;
communities of pre-Christianity European Alps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:58.740
it was a good reminder to be vigilant; that hubris&nbsp;
could be the first step towards accidental death.

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:02.100
Similar legends appear often in old&nbsp;
European folklore, usually repeating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:06.240
the central theme of the creator killed&nbsp;
by his creation. The Golem of Prague is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:11.160
a well-known example from the 16th century. It&nbsp;
is said Judah Loew ben Bezalel, the late rabbi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:15.180
made the Golem from clay and brought it to life&nbsp;
to defend the Prague ghettos from anti-semitic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:19.380
attacks. The legend ends familiarly, with&nbsp;
the Golem going on a murderous rampage.

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:24.180
Another example is Mary Shelley’s Frankenstein,&nbsp;
where a Swiss doctor brings to life a man pieced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:28.440
together from the body parts of the dead only&nbsp;
to lose control of the creature to, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:33.780
a murderous rampage. James Cameron’s Terminator 2:&nbsp;
Judgment Day can also be considered, as it shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:38.580
an android created by the evil corporation Skynet&nbsp;
reprogrammed to fight back against them. More&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:42.900
recently, The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild&nbsp;
puts the player against huge mechanical beasts and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:47.340
autonomous guardians which were initially created&nbsp;
to protect their civilization, but were hijacked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:52.200
by the evil Calamity Ganon and yeah, I think you&nbsp;
see the pattern at this point. Murderous rampage.

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:56.820
While The Golem, Frankenstein, T2 and Breath&nbsp;
of the Wild all have the motif seen in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.820 --> 00:09:00.960
pre-Christian folklore narrative, they also&nbsp;
all illustrate the effect of the post-medieval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:05.280
cultural shift from superstition to the use of&nbsp;
reason, the scientific method, and progress.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:10.020
This is the nature of folklore, the legend spreads&nbsp;
and takes the shape of the culture it’s retold in.

00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:15.000
In the 2019 film adaptation, Scary Stories to Tell&nbsp;
in the Dark, the reality is a little twisted. Set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:19.800
in the 1960s, the in-universe author of the&nbsp;
scary stories is Sarah Bellows, a girl with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:24.420
albinism who perished after being locked away by&nbsp;
her family. After a hapless group of kids stumbles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:28.874
upon her magic book, she terrorizes their town&nbsp;
by bringing her original scary stories to life.

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:32.940
In this variation, we first meet Harold&nbsp;
hanging out in a field where local jock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:36.660
Tommy Milner takes out his frustrations on the&nbsp;
scarecrow each time he passes by.

00:09:36.660 --> 00:09:38.280
– [Tommy] Take that!

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:39.720
– [Zac] The fields, and Harold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:43.440
belong to Tommy’s family and Tommy is&nbsp;
just begging for an ironic punishment.

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:47.580
As I mentioned earlier, in modern popular&nbsp;
media, there’s usually a reason for supernatural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:51.720
occurrences beginning. In the film, “Harold”&nbsp;
comes alive as the ghost of Sarah Bellows writes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:56.100
each scary story in this book. After the “Harold”&nbsp;
story appears on the page, Harold gains sentience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.100 --> 00:09:59.700
and Lt. Peaked-In-High-School sucker&nbsp;
punches him on his way through the fields.

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:02.400
– [Tommy] Eat sh*t, Harold.

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:06.000
– The actual story shown on screen seems&nbsp;
similar to the Alvin Schwartz version,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:10.380
with the name Thomas replaced with Tommy and a&nbsp;
couple details from Tommy Milner’s story thrown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:14.760
in, such as the fact that he was going to deliver&nbsp;
eggs, which Mrs. Milner had just asked him to do.

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:17.280
– You were supposed to deliver&nbsp;
eggs to the Wolvertons!

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:21.180
– When Tommy passes by Harold’s spot again,&nbsp;
he finds nothing but an empty post,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:24.240
and he hears something stalking&nbsp;
him before this happens.

00:10:24.929 --> 00:10:26.360
(stinger)

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:30.540
With a crack, Harold moves right&nbsp;
before Tommy’s eyes and comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.540 --> 00:10:33.600
after him. Tommy is able to find a&nbsp;
pitchfork, but it does very little,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:37.200
and Harold just pulls it out and jabs&nbsp;
it right back into Tommy’s blindside.

00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:41.940
But instead of blood, Tommy sprouts twigs of&nbsp;
hay from all parts of his body and he quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:46.620
transforms into a scarecrow himself. His fate&nbsp;
seems to tell the same old moral in a new way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.620 --> 00:10:50.280
with Tommy being forced to become the&nbsp;
object that he for so long abused.

00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:53.040
Harold’s story in the film&nbsp;
adaptation doesn’t continue the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:57.120
old legend’s finale of human skinning, but&nbsp;
the gruesome fate is no fairy tale ending.

00:10:57.120 --> 00:10:58.980
– [Stella] It’s what Tommy was wearing last night.

00:10:58.980 --> 00:11:03.000
– It’s clear, from the movie adaptation and&nbsp;
the outcry when the original illustrations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:07.140
were replaced in 2011, that folklore and&nbsp;
story-telling still have some place in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:10.260
streaming world. Harold is probably&nbsp;
one of the more famous scarecrows in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:13.800
literature and he’s never even called a&nbsp;
“scarecrow” in the story. And his iconic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:17.160
appearance in the movie and prominent&nbsp;
showcasing in all its marketing material&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:20.940
will ensure that future generations&nbsp;
are warned, by the tale of “Harold”.

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:25.560
If you want to learn about other horror icons&nbsp;
with folklore influences, such as The Pale Lady,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:29.340
check out the playlist on the left. And if&nbsp;
you love horror, remember to subscribe to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.340 --> 00:11:33.240
CZsWorld for new horrors every week, ring&nbsp;
the deathbell and select all notifications,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:37.498
and I will see you in the next&nbsp;
one. Assuming we both survive.

00:11:37.716 --> 00:11:39.716
(spooky music)

